    s__CARE_COSTS_MEANS(py_str("The council looks at the person's own income, savings and property, not the family's: in England, savings over £23,250 means paying the full cost, under £14,250 the council ignores them, and in between it counts £1 a week of income for every £250 - so a house is not sold on the first day, and a deferred payment agreement lets the council put a charge on it and take the money from the estate instead. The house is ignored completely while a husband, wife or civil partner lives there, and it is always ignored for care in your own home. Ask for the financial assessment and the care needs assessment in writing, and get the free check from Age UK or Citizens Advice before signing anything."));
    if std::env::var_os("PYBOT_TRACE_INIT").is_some() { eprintln!("SET knowledge.money._CARE_COSTS_HELP"); }
    s__CARE_COSTS_HELP(py_str("Claim what is paid whatever the savings first, then let the council's assessment do the rest: Attendance Allowance if the person needs help day to day and is over State Pension age (PIP daily living below it), Pension Credit and the council tax reduction that comes with it, and NHS continuing healthcare if the need is mostly health. The council part works on the person's own money: in England, savings over £23,250 means paying the full cost, under £14,250 the council ignores them, and in between it counts £1 a week for every £250, so a house is not sold on the first day and a deferred payment agreement puts a charge on it and takes the money from the estate instead. Ask for the financial assessment and the care needs assessment in writing, and get the free check from Age UK or Citizens Advice before signing anything."));
